.TH DATE 1
.SH NAME
date \- print the date and time, or set the clock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
[\ \fB+\fIformat\fR\ ]
.br
.B date
.I mmddhhmm\fR[[\fIcc\fR]\fIyy\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without an argument,
.B date
prints the date and time, in the local time zone:
.PP
.nf
	Tue Oct 13 12:00:00 PDT 1998
.fi
.PP
With an argument that begins with +, it prints the date and time in
that format: each character as it is, but for a % and the letter after
it, which give
.TP 6
.B %m
the month, 01 to 12
.TP
.B %d
the day of the month, 01 to 31
.TP
.B %y
the year in two digits, 00 to 99
.TP
.B %Y
the year in four digits
.TP
.B %D
the date as mm/dd/yy
.TP
.B %H
the hour, 00 to 23
.TP
.B %M
the minute, 00 to 59
.TP
.B %S
the second, 00 to 59
.TP
.B %T
the time as HH:MM:SS
.TP
.B %r
the time as hh:MM:SS AM or PM
.TP
.B %j
the day of the year, 001 to 366
.TP
.B %w
the day of the week, Sunday being 0
.TP
.B %a
the day of the week, Sun to Sat
.TP
.B %h
the month, Jan to Dec
.TP
.B %n
a newline
.TP
.B %t
a tab
.TP
.B %%
a %
.PP
and a newline ends what it prints. Quote a format with spaces in it:
.PP
.nf
	$ date '+%h %d, %Y, at %r'
	Oct 13, 1998, at 12:00:00 PM
.fi
.PP
Given a date and time instead, as month, day, hour and minute, two
digits each, and the year, root's
.B date
sets the clock to it, at second 0, and prints it. Without a year, it
stays this year's; a year in two digits is 1970 to 1999 from 70 to 99,
and 2000 to 2069 from 00 to 69; and a year in four digits is that year,
up to 2106.
.PP
.nf
	# date 101312002026
	Tue Oct 13 12:00:00 PDT 2026
.fi
.PP
The time given is local time, daylight time when daylight time is in
force on that date. Setting the clock writes the time before and after,
as "old time" and "new time", in /etc/utmp and at the end of /etc/wtmp.
Only the first argument is read.
.PP
This
.B date
takes the place of XENIX's own, which it prints the same as, in every
format, up to the end of 1999. XENIX's takes a two-digit year only;
and, taking 2000 for a common year, it prints any date from
February 29, 2000 on a day ahead, and the year in %y as :0 for 2000 and
<6 for 2026. XENIX's
.B tar
shares the mistake.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 8
TZ
the time zone, for example PST8PDT; see
.BR ctime (3).
Without it, or empty, PST8PDT, as for XENIX's own date.
.SH FILES
.TP 16
/etc/utmp
who is logged in, and when the clock was last set
.TP
/etc/wtmp
the logins, and the times the clock was set
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
date: bad conversion
The argument to set the clock is not a date and time that exists: not 8,
10 or 12 digits; a month, day, hour or minute out of range, including a
day the month does not have; or a time before 1970 or after the clock's
end in February 2106.
.TP
date: bad format character \- \fIc\fR
A format has a % and a letter it does not know.
.TP
date: no permission
Only root can set the clock.
.TP
date: no TOY clock
For
.BR \-u .
XENIX's own
.B date
uses a time-of-year clock with \-u, and on an XT finds none; this one
cannot read one at all.
.PP
The exit status is 0, or 2 after any of these.
.SH SEE ALSO
ctime(3), who(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B date
command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. Its formats with + are those of
System V.
.SH AUTHORS
This date was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
XENIX's kernel has not been tried past January 19, 2038, when its count
of seconds passes 2147483647, the largest a signed long holds.
.PP
XENIX's own programs that print dates with their own copy of the C
library, such as
.BR tar ,
print them a day ahead from February 29, 2000 on, as its date did.
